                                 BEFORE

Amaia had fourteen when they put the canoes in. By the third night
she had eleven.
   Two went at a channel in the mangroves that had been open at noon
and was not open after dark. The third went to fever, an old man who
asked for water, then for his wife, who was not with them, then for
nothing. Amaia stopped saying their names after that. She would say
them later, every year of her life, but not on the river.
   Her brother Dairon had the lead canoe. He hadn’t spoken since the
second night except to say left or wait. Yara rode behind him with the
cemí wrapped against her chest, in a cloth that had been soaked and
dried so many times it had gone stiﬀ as bark. Beheto sat in the second
canoe with the drum between his knees, his chest whistling on every
breath. He was the oldest of them now. The third canoe held the three
children the behique called the lit ones. They hadn’t cried in two days.
Amaia would have given a great deal to hear one of them cry.
   She had seen the blue come oﬀ them three times: twice in fever, once
in the half second after lightning. It was the color of the inside of a
shell, and it was gone before she could point at it, and there was no one
left she could have pointed it out to.
   Her son slept against her ribs. He was not yet two.
   On the bank behind them, the soldiers had stopped following. One
of them lifted a pole with a brass bell hung from it and rang it, not



