hard. Amaia felt it in her back teeth. There was no song in it. The three
children turned their heads toward it at the same time, and the blue
lifted oﬀ their arms and leaned out over the black water toward the
bank.
    A second man sat on a stump with a book open on his knee. He
didn’t look up at the canoes. He wrote something down, then
something under it.
    “There’s a way under the roots,” said the girl of seven, to the water.
    Dairon turned the bow without asking how she knew. The hull
shrieked along the roots on both sides. Somebody’s paddle snapped.
Then the channel let them through, into air that smelled of salt from
the wrong direction.
    “They know where we were,” said the boy of nine, his head tilted
toward the bell. “They don’t know where we are.”
    Beheto put both palms on the drum. He leaned his weight into the
drum, sending the sound through the hull, the water, and the roots.
Amaia couldn’t have told you what it asked. The current shifted under
them. Nothing rose up and nothing parted. The canoes simply began to
move a little faster than the paddles were moving them.
    “It’s turning with us,” her son said into her ribs, in a voice too old
for him. “Turn with it.”
    She turned with it. Behind her, on the bank, the man with the book
ﬁnished his line and closed it.

